\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Kalman-smoothed shocks (in $\sigma $ units), 2018--2025. Both series are evaluated through the same linear Kalman smoother; only the parameter vector differs. Blue dashed: linear posterior mean; red solid: surrogate posterior mean. Gray band: COVID window (2020Q1--2021Q2). At the linear posterior, risk-premium shocks peak at $56\sigma $. At the surrogate posterior, the halved $\sigma _b$ forces even larger normalized shocks---the surrogate's parameters are structurally incompatible with a linear filter. The 30-nat log-likelihood improvement under the regime-switching likelihood (Table~\ref {tab:extended_sample_posterior}) confirms that the nonlinear propagation channel, not parameter recalibration alone, accounts for the better fit.}}{35}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:covid_shock_comparison}{{7}{35}{Kalman-smoothed shocks (in $\sigma $ units), 2018--2025. Both series are evaluated through the same linear Kalman smoother; only the parameter vector differs. Blue dashed: linear posterior mean; red solid: surrogate posterior mean. Gray band: COVID window (2020Q1--2021Q2). At the linear posterior, risk-premium shocks peak at $56\sigma $. At the surrogate posterior, the halved $\sigma _b$ forces even larger normalized shocks---the surrogate's parameters are structurally incompatible with a linear filter. The 30-nat log-likelihood improvement under the regime-switching likelihood (Table~\ref {tab:extended_sample_posterior}) confirms that the nonlinear propagation channel, not parameter recalibration alone, accounts for the better fit}{figure.caption.16}{}}
