\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Absolute magnitude of the FOM--ROM1 gap by equation block across shock scales 0.1--1.5. Investment/capital dominates at every scale; during ZLB-binding episodes at moderate scales (0.8--1.2$\sigma $) the investment share is 88--92 percent, exceeding the next-largest block by a factor of eight. At the most extreme scale (1.5$\sigma $) the binding share falls to 37 percent, though only 3 binding periods are observed.}}{102}{figure.caption.55}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gap_magnitude_vs_scale}{{8}{102}{Absolute magnitude of the FOM--ROM1 gap by equation block across shock scales 0.1--1.5. Investment/capital dominates at every scale; during ZLB-binding episodes at moderate scales (0.8--1.2$\sigma $) the investment share is 88--92 percent, exceeding the next-largest block by a factor of eight. At the most extreme scale (1.5$\sigma $) the binding share falls to 37 percent, though only 3 binding periods are observed}{figure.caption.55}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Equation-block decomposition of the FOM--ROM1 gap in the Gal\'i (2015) model, which shares the ELB and Kimball structure with the HLT model but lacks investment. The absolute gap magnitude is an order of magnitude smaller than in the HLT model at every shock scale, confirming that the investment channel---not the ELB or Kimball pricing---drives the nonlinearity.}}{103}{figure.caption.56}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gali_block_decomposition}{{9}{103}{Equation-block decomposition of the FOM--ROM1 gap in the Gal\'i (2015) model, which shares the ELB and Kimball structure with the HLT model but lacks investment. The absolute gap magnitude is an order of magnitude smaller than in the HLT model at every shock scale, confirming that the investment channel---not the ELB or Kimball pricing---drives the nonlinearity}{figure.caption.56}{}}
